# kills: the landscape deck strip scrolls rather than clips, at both ends
# suite: node --test --test-name-pattern ^the.landscape.deck.strip.scrolls.rather.than.clips..at.both.ends$ tests/e2e.test.js
# .decks goes overflow-x:auto -> clip. This is the defect the landscape
# comment's old "no scroll at all" claim would have hidden: the strip really
# is wider than the viewport at 844x390 and 667x375 with a custom deck, and
# `auto` is the only reason that is a scroll and not a clip. Every existing
# landscape assertion - header height, chrome budget, card width, body
# scrollWidth - is unmoved, because the chips lay out at exactly the same
# widths; what changes is that 41px and 218px of chip become untappable
# (measured: maxScroll 0, the last chip ending 217.7px past the right edge).
#
# `clip` and not `hidden`, deliberately: an overflow:hidden box in Chrome is
# still programmatically scrollable, so scrollLeft moves, every end lands
# flush, and the mutant SURVIVES - which is worth knowing, because "hidden is
# just auto without the scrollbar" is exactly the wrong intuition to have
# about this rule.
diff --git a/index.html b/index.html
index 77d0b3d..8fba33b 100644
--- a/index.html
+++ b/index.html
@@ -104,7 +104,7 @@ h1{font-family:Marcellus, serif; font-weight:400; font-size:clamp(20px,5.5vw,27p
 /* The chip row scrolls horizontally and never wraps; auto margins on the end
    chips keep it centred while it fits, without making either end unreachable
    once it overflows. The right edge fades so a cut-off chip reads as "more". */
-.decks{display:flex; gap:var(--sp-2); flex-wrap:nowrap; overflow-x:auto; scrollbar-width:none;
+.decks{display:flex; gap:var(--sp-2); flex-wrap:nowrap; overflow-x:clip; scrollbar-width:none;
   -webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch; overscroll-behavior:contain;
   -webkit-mask-image:linear-gradient(90deg,#000 0 calc(100% - 22px), transparent 100%);
   mask-image:linear-gradient(90deg,#000 0 calc(100% - 22px), transparent 100%)}
